{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>באור פני מלך חיים ורצונו כעב מלקוש (משלי טז, טו)</b>" "<b>King Solomon,</b> of blessed memory, explained in this verse (Proverbs 16:14) the benefit that comes to a person through the king's favor and the brightness of his countenance. He compared his favor to the late rain in a cloud (עב מלקוש), which everyone desires and longs for. So too in another place he compared it to dew, as he said concerning this (Proverbs 16:15): ״נהם ככפיר זעף מלך וכטל על עשב רצונו״. And the reason he compared his favor to two things, to rain and to dew, is that the benefit of rain is for the future — its benefit is not seen immediately, but at harvest time the benefit becomes known through the increase of grain; whereas the benefit of dew comes immediately. So too the king's favor sometimes brings benefit immediately and sometimes for the future.\n\nAnd thus King Solomon taught us in several verses of the book of Proverbs the harm that comes to a person from the king's anger, even though he does not exact punishment, and is not even about to carry out his anger from potentiality to actuality — all the more so if he intends to take vengeance on those who provoke him. He explained in general terms that if he is angry but does not exact punishment, the one who provoked him is called dread and trembling, like one who hears the roar of a lion's growl and the sound of a panther — he trembles and is terrified even though the beast will not ultimately harm him. Concerning this he said: ״נהם ככפיר זעף מלך״. But if he is angry and intends to take vengeance, that is death, and concerning this he said (Proverbs 16): ״חמת מלך מלאכי מות״. Just as his wrath is death, so his favor and the light of his countenance are life; and concerning this he said here: ״באור פני מלך חיים״.\n\nAnd Solomon's intention in all this was to argue a fortiori (קל וחומר) concerning the King of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He: if with a king of flesh and blood, life is bound up with the light of his countenance and benefit is found in his favor — like the benefit of the earth found in the late rain (עב מלקוש), as it is written (Isaiah 55:10) ״כי אם הרוה את הארץ והולידה והצמיחה״ — all the more so with the Holy One, blessed be He, whose true life depends on His favor and the brightness of His countenance, as it is written (Psalms 30:6) ״כי רגע באפו חיים ברצונו״. And concerning this Solomon, of blessed memory, said: ״באור פני מלך חיים״, coming to teach us that life is bound up with the revelation of the Shekhinah.\n\nNow we find that the revelation of the Shekhinah comes in three forms. The first, for speech, to command the prophet concerning a commandment of the Torah. The second, to defend His righteous servants, as it is written (Numbers 14:10) ״ויאמרו כל העדה לרגום אותם באבנים וכבוד ה׳ נראה באהל מועד״. The third, by way of favor and brightness of countenance — like the revelation of the Shekhinah that was in the Tabernacle, and the revelation of the Shekhinah upon Abraham: the revelation of the Shekhinah that was in the Tabernacle, as it is written (Leviticus 9:23) ״ויצאו ויברכו את העם וירא כבוד ה׳ אל כל העם״ — for because they exerted themselves in the commandment of the Tabernacle, they merited the revelation of the Shekhinah; the revelation of the Shekhinah to Abraham — because he exerted himself in the commandment of circumcision, he merited the revelation of the Shekhinah. And this is ״באור פני מלך חיים״, for the word חיים (life) embraces healing (רפואה), from the language of (Isaiah 38:9) ״ויחי מחליו״, and it is written (ibid.) ״ותחלימני והחייני״. So too Abraham received healing through the revelation of the Shekhinah: he was ill from the commandment of circumcision and was then healed when the Holy One, blessed be He, appeared to him. And this is what is written." "<b>And YHWH appeared to him in the oaks of Mamre, while he was sitting at the entrance of the tent in the heat of the day.</b> This section is attached and joined to the section above; therefore it says ״וירא אליו״ — meaning, to Abraham, who was already mentioned. For had it not been so, it would have been proper to say ״וירא ה׳ אל אברהם״, just as it said earlier ״וירא ה׳ אל אברם״. But it says ״וירא אליו״, and the intention is to say that in the merit of circumcision HaShem appeared to him; and so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: לבקר את החולה (to visit the sick)." "<b>In the oaks of Mamre.</b> He informed us of the place where he was circumcised, and there He gave him his reward in that very place, through the revelation of the Shekhinah. Now we find that the Shekhinah is revealed to the prophets — in fire, in wind, in water, and in earth. In fire, to Moses at the bush, as it is written (Exodus 3:2) ״וירא והנה הסנה בוער באש״; also to all Israel at Sinai, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:36) ״ודבריו שמעת מתוך האש״, and it is written (Exodus 24:17) ״ומראה כבוד ה׳ כאש אוכלת״. In wind, to Elijah, as it is written (II Kings 2:1) ״ויהי בהעלות ה׳ את אליהו בסערה השמים״ and so forth, and ״והנה ה׳ עובר ורוח גדולה וחזק מפרק הרים״ and so forth (I Kings 19:11). And likewise in Job (Job 38:1) ״ויען ה׳ את איוב מן הסערה״. In water, to Ezekiel, as it is written (Ezekiel 1:1) ״ויהי בשלשים שנה ברביעי בחמשה לחדש ואני בתוך הגולה על נהר כבר״, and it is written (ibid.) ״ואשמע את קול כנפיהם כקול מים רבים״. In earth, as it is written (Isaiah 6:3) ״מלא כל הארץ כבודו״, and it is written (Ezekiel 43:2) ״והארץ האירה מכבודו״. And here He revealed Himself to Abraham in a tree (אילן), and that is what is written: ״באלוני ממרא״.\n\nNow Mamre was one of his associates, as it is written concerning Abraham (Genesis 14:13) ״והוא שוכן באלוני ממרא האמורי אחי אשכול ואחי ענר והם בעלי ברית אברם״. He could have said ״בערבות ממרא״ but he said ״באלוני״ to teach you that there were trees there. And from what it says ״והשענו תחת העץ״, we learn that there was only one tree there.\n\nAnd why did He reveal Himself to Abraham in a tree? Because He foretold him that he would father a child in his old age, like this tree concerning which it is written (Job 14:7–9) ״כי יש לעץ תקוה אם יכרת ועוד יחליף ויונקתו לא תחדל אם יזקין בארץ שרשו ובעפר ימות גזעו מריח מים יפריח ועשה קציר כמו נטע״. And furthermore, the righteous are compared to a tree, as it is written (Psalms 1:3) ״והיה כעץ שתול על פלגי מים״. And so explained our master Hananel, of blessed memory." "<b>While he was sitting at the entrance of the tent in the heat of the day.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): because he was weakened from the commandment of circumcision, he was sitting there during the heat of the day, for the sun heals with its wings." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> כחום היוב — 'in the heat of the day' — it was the fourth hour of the day, which is the time for eating, and he was there waiting for guests." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah.</b> והוא יושב פתח האהל — 'and he was sitting at the entrance of the tent' — alludes to Knesset Yisrael, upon which he would set his mind and gaze, for since it began with ״וירא אליו ה׳״ — 'and YHWH appeared to him' — and we know that the designated Name did not appear to the Patriarchs except as El Shaddai, therefore it was necessary to say ״והוא יושב פתח האהל״ — and understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:29.240740955Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}